            {
              "id": "8eed4c59-8bef-354b-a56f-7ee98c7a2d34",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions when using the strncpy function. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory areas, which is not allowed for strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer sizes and the lack of explicit null-termination after the copy further increase the risk. The destination buffer's capacity is not explicitly known, and the count argument is a literal value (256), which may not match the actual buffer size. All these factors contribute to a high confidence in the finding's validity.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the strncpy call, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a safe copy strategy such as copying to a temporary buffer first. Additionally, always check that both source and destination pointers are non-null before copying, and ensure the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to prevent unterminated strings.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_msg != nullptr && msg_ != nullptr && this->m_msg != msg_) {\n    strncpy(this->m_msg, msg_, 256);\n    this->m_msg[255] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nIf overlap cannot be ruled out, consider copying to a temporary buffer:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_msg != nullptr && msg_ != nullptr) {\n    char temp[256];\n    strncpy(temp, msg_, 256);\n    temp[255] = '\\0';\n    memcpy(this->m_msg, temp, 256);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "71159834-69f2-3857-82bd-8fc325e04a1b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory through data flow, which is not allowed by the C standard for strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer size or null checks, and the lack of explicit null-termination after the copy, further increase the risk. The destination buffer's capacity is not explicitly known, and the count argument is a literal value (128), which may not match the actual buffer size, compounding the risk. All these factors together make this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the strncpy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a safe copy strategy such as copying to a temporary buffer first. Additionally, add explicit null pointer checks for both the source and destination to prevent null pointer dereference. Finally, ensure that the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_arg_name != nullptr && arg_name_ != nullptr && this->m_arg_name != arg_name_) {\n    // Optionally, use a temporary buffer if overlap is possible\n    char temp[128];\n    strncpy(temp, arg_name_, 128);\n    temp[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n    strncpy(this->m_arg_name, temp, 128);\n    this->m_arg_name[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nIf the destination and source are guaranteed not to overlap, you can simply add null checks and ensure null-termination:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_arg_name != nullptr && arg_name_ != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(this->m_arg_name, arg_name_, 128);\n    this->m_arg_name[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "616f6250-ddf4-35c5-8cfb-c53c8ab5c686",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the given code snippet, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow if the input string represents a number larger than `INT_MAX`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the language. For instance, in C++, you can use `std::stoi()` instead of `atoi()`. Always validate and sanitize user inputs to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `std::stoi()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <string>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::string getenv_str = std::getenv(\"ENV_VAR\");\nint getenv_int = std::stoi(getenv_str);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdlib\n- string\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "371585ab-d471-33f0-a812-9d6c28b4a438",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory areas, which is not allowed by the C standard for strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer sizes or null checks, and the lack of explicit null-termination after the copy, further increase the risk. The destination buffer's capacity is not known, and the count argument is a literal value (128), which may not match the actual buffer size. All these factors contribute to a high confidence that this is a real and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the strncpy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function such as memmove, which is designed to handle overlapping regions. Additionally, add explicit null pointer checks for both the source and destination to prevent null pointer dereference. If the destination buffer is a fixed-size array, ensure the copy does not exceed its capacity and explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_function_name != nullptr && function_name_ != nullptr) {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove instead of strncpy\n    memmove(this->m_function_name, function_name_, 128);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination if m_function_name is a string\n    this->m_function_name[127] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nIf you know the actual size of the destination buffer, replace 128 with the correct size, and always ensure null-termination for string buffers.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "25be337e-a9f1-3c8f-bf23-1667e628a864",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe or insecure. In this case, the function `getenv()` is used, which can lead to security issues. The `getenv()` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name passed as input. This can lead to potential security risks such as buffer overflow, or information disclosure if the environment variable contains sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `getenv()` function in your code. If you need to use environment variables, consider using safer alternatives that are less prone to security risks. Always validate and sanitize the input to these functions to prevent potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv()`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::getenv()` function from the C++ Standard Library, which is considered safer. Here is an example of how to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nconst char* value = std::getenv(\"name\");\nif (value == nullptr) {\n    // The environment variable does not exist.\n} else {\n    // The environment variable exists and its value is stored in 'value'.\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This is a C++ Standard Library that provides several general purpose functions, including a safer version of the `getenv()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "87a3fda7-af65-3c7c-aba3-f5b56b02c6f2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions when using the strncpy function. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory areas, which is not allowed by strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination are non-null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior or crashes. The absence of explicit null-termination after the copy and the lack of bounds checks or guards make this issue more severe. All these factors together confirm that this is a real and actionable security or correctness problem.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the strncpy call, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a safe approach such as copying to a temporary buffer or using memmove only if absolutely necessary. Additionally, check that both source and destination pointers are not null before copying, and explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after the copy to prevent unterminated strings.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_function_filename != nullptr && function_filename_ != nullptr && this->m_function_filename != function_filename_) {\n    strncpy(this->m_function_filename, function_filename_, 128);\n    this->m_function_filename[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nIf overlap cannot be ruled out, use a temporary buffer:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_function_filename != nullptr && function_filename_ != nullptr) {\n    char temp[128];\n    strncpy(temp, function_filename_, 128);\n    temp[127] = '\\0';\n    strncpy(this->m_function_filename, temp, 128);\n    this->m_function_filename[127] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b307aa99-94a4-3e4a-819f-d97fc1b62bf4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a prohibited C function due to its potential to cause security vulnerabilities. The `atoi()` function converts a string to an integer and it does not perform any error checking. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform error checking. In C++, you can use the `std::stoi()` function which throws an exception if the conversion fails. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `std::stoi()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\ntry {\n    layer_mode = static_cast<rocsparse_layer_mode>(std::stoi(str_layer_mode));\n} catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n} catch (std::out_of_range& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `rocsparse` for the `rocsparse_layer_mode` type.\n- `cstdlib` for the `atoi()` function (in the original code).\n- `string` for the `std::stoi()` function (in the fixed code).\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9c0265d9-0b43-348d-8828-65e283763b30",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the function `getenv()` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv()` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This string can be overwritten by subsequent calls to `getenv()`, `setenv()`, or `putenv()`. Moreover, the returned string can be a potential security risk as it can be manipulated by an attacker to point to malicious code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv()` function directly. Instead, use a safer alternative that does not have these vulnerabilities. If you must use `getenv()`, ensure that the returned string is not directly used without proper validation and sanitization. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of directly using the returned string from `getenv()`, copy it to a local buffer and use the local copy. This can be done using `strncpy()` function. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\nchar* str_layer_mode;\nchar local_copy[256];\nif((str_layer_mode = getenv(\"ROCSPARSE_LAYER\")) != NULL) {\n    strncpy(local_copy, str_layer_mode, sizeof(local_copy));\n    local_copy[sizeof(local_copy) - 1] = '\\0';\n    // Use local_copy instead of str_layer_mode\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy()` is used to copy the string returned by `getenv()` to a local buffer. The size of the buffer is specified to prevent buffer overflow. The last character is explicitly set to null to ensure that the string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `cstdlib` (for `getenv()` function)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7d1d8128-5bee-3a7e-8e66-ef62ee5f14d4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow if the input is not properly validated or sanitized.\n\nThe `atoi()` function converts a string to an integer. It does not perform any error checking, so if the string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero. This can lead to unexpected behavior if the string is not a valid integer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `atoi()` and instead use functions that perform error checking, such as `strtol()`. You should also validate and sanitize all input to ensure it is in the expected format and range.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `strtol()`, which performs error checking:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar *endptr;\nlong int ndim = strtol(argv[1], &endptr, 10);\n\nif (errno == ERANGE) {\n    // Handle overflow or underflow\n} else if (endptr == argv[1]) {\n    // Handle invalid input\n} else {\n    // Handle additional error checking\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This library is required for the `strtol()` function.\n- `<cerrno>`: This library is required to check for errors returned by `strtol()`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-681: Incorrect Conversion between Numeric Types](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/681.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5cf8c386-96f1-3a57-8329-89ac4b79b135",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `atoi()` function is used, which is a prohibited function because it does not perform any error checking and can lead to integer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform error checking. For instance, instead of `atoi()`, you can use `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `strtol()` or `sscanf()`. Here is an example of how to use `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar *endptr;\nlong int batch_size = strtol(argv[3], &endptr, 10);\n\nif (errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle overflow/underflow\n} else if (endptr == argv[3]) {\n    // handle no digits were found\n} else {\n    // use batch_size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `strtol()`\n- `<cerrno>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ffadc2a1-7faf-3980-a207-ec7a9220e05c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard C library function that converts a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not handle errors well. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()`. Instead, use functions that can handle errors better, such as `strtol()`, `sscanf()`, or `stoi()` in C++. These functions provide mechanisms to check if the entire string was converted, if any characters were left over, or if the converted value was out of range.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi()` with `strtol()`, `sscanf()`, or `stoi()`:\n\n```cpp\nchar *end;\nlong int trials = strtol(argv[2], &end, 10);\nif (end == argv[2] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h` for `strtol()`\n- `errno.h` for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1d30594-292c-3256-98a1-dd78050aa825",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `atoi()` function is used, which is a prohibited function because it does not perform any error checking and can lead to integer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform error checking. For instance, instead of `atoi()`, you can use `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `strtol()` or `sscanf()`. Here is an example of how to use `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar *endptr;\nlong int batch_size = strtol(argv[3], &endptr, 10);\n\nif (errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle overflow/underflow\n} else if (endptr == argv[3]) {\n    // handle no digits were found\n} else {\n    // use batch_size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `strtol()`\n- `<cerrno>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cdb6a157-dcba-3995-ac1a-ff6aa9dcf956",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions. The analysis detected that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior when using memory copy functions like memcpy. The destination and source are both pointers, and there is no evidence that they are guaranteed to be non-overlapping. Additionally, there are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap. The source and destination are also both non-null at the call site, so the main risk is the potential for overlapping memory regions, which is a well-known cause of undefined behavior in C/C++ memory operations.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. The `memmove` function is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(rev, v, sizeof(v));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the memory copy will work correctly even if the source and destination overlap, eliminating the risk of undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "61b69c29-d022-3dc5-9ec2-590684d0b811",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in question is `strcat(end, \".0\")`. Here, `strcat` is used to append the string \".0\" to the string `end`. If the buffer `end` is not large enough to hold the additional characters, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat` or `strlcat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` or `strlcat`. Here is an example using `strncat`:\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming end is a char array with sufficient space\nstrncat(end, \".0\", sizeof(end) - strlen(end) - 1);\n```\n\nThis code will append at most `sizeof(end) - strlen(end) - 1` characters from \".0\" to `end`, thus ensuring that `end` does not overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat`, `strncat`, `strlen` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ec1407ee-bbf2-322f-931a-740cecb5cb21",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `atoi()` function is used, which is a prohibited function because it does not perform any error checking and can lead to integer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform error checking. For instance, instead of `atoi()`, you can use `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `strtol()` or `sscanf()`. Here is an example of how to use `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar *endptr;\nlong int batch_size = strtol(argv[3], &endptr, 10);\n\nif (errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle overflow/underflow\n} else if (endptr == argv[3]) {\n    // handle no digits were found\n} else {\n    // use batch_size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `strtol()`\n- `<cerrno>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
