            {
              "id": "0be5383e-abb8-3a45-8625-2209c8e51272",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of the `strlen` function in the C++ programming language. The `strlen` function is used to determine the length of a string, but it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. In the provided code snippet, the `strlen` function is used as the third argument to the `strncmp` function, which compares two strings up to a specified number of characters. If the `name` string is not null-terminated, the `strlen` function could return a value that exceeds the actual size of the `name` buffer, leading to a buffer overflow when `strncmp` is called.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that strings in C++ are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing strings with a null character at the end, or by manually adding a null character at the end of a string after it has been modified. Additionally, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that do not rely on null-termination, such as `strnlen` and `strncmp`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer version of the code snippet would look like this:\n\n```cpp\n0 == strncmp(name, archname, strnlen(name, sizeof(name)))\n```\n\nIn this version, the `strnlen` function is used instead of `strlen`. The `strnlen` function returns the length of the string or the maximum number of characters to consider, whichever is smaller. This prevents a buffer overflow even if the `name` string is not null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strncmp` and `strnlen` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ee562824-d785-3a7b-9804-3b7e78e9ce20",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the filename is not properly validated or controlled. An attacker could potentially manipulate the filename to read or write files that they should not have access to.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to perform proper input validation and error checking. In the case of `fopen`, ensure that the filename is properly validated and controlled, and that error checking is performed to handle cases where the file cannot be opened.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream f;\nf.open(filename);\nif (!f.is_open()) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the C++ `ifstream` class is used instead of `fopen`. This class provides more safety features and better error handling.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n* `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n* [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n* [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0f3bf31a-0757-3713-948a-995b89ee41ea",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable or easily guessable values. This can be exploited by attackers to predict the outcome of the random number generator, leading to potential security risks.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `rand()` is a weak random number generator as it uses a linear congruential generator which is not suitable for cryptographic purposes.\n\n```cpp\nrng = col_begin + rand() % col_nnz;\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, the `<random>` library provides several such generators. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code snippet using the Mersenne Twister engine, a strong random number generator provided by the `<random>` library in C++.\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(col_begin, col_nnz);\n\nrng = dis(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "0f3bf31a-0757-3713-948a-995b89ee41ea"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5abff24c-83d3-3d4a-ab96-b40f92bb7f7e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of the `strlen` function in the C++ programming language. The `strlen` function is used to determine the length of a string, but it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. In the provided code snippet, the `strlen` function is used as the third argument to the `strncmp` function, which compares two strings up to a specified number of characters. If the `name` string is not null-terminated, the `strlen` function could return a value that exceeds the actual size of the `name` buffer, leading to a buffer overflow when `strncmp` is called.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that strings in C++ are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing strings with a null character at the end, or by manually adding a null character at the end of a string after it has been modified. Additionally, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that do not rely on null-termination, such as `strnlen` and `strncmp`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer version of the code snippet would look like this:\n\n```cpp\n0 == strncmp(name, archname, strnlen(name, sizeof(name)))\n```\n\nIn this version, the `strnlen` function is used instead of `strlen`. The `strnlen` function returns the length of the string or the maximum number of characters to consider, whichever is smaller. This prevents a buffer overflow even if the `name` string is not null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strncmp` and `strnlen` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
