            {
              "id": "58429419-ed8c-31b5-a766-862bf32b3ac2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size parameter passed to `malloc` is not properly validated or is manipulated in such a way that it can cause a buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other memory corruption issues. This can lead to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, or even code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate the size parameter before passing it to `malloc`.\n2. Avoid using user-supplied or externally influenced numbers directly in memory allocation functions.\n3. Use safer memory management functions if possible, such as those provided by the C++ Standard Library (e.g., `new` and `delete`).\n4. Implement error checking after every `malloc` call. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer, which should be checked.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to fix the vulnerability in the given code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming m_ is an integer\nint m_ = 10;\n\n// Validate m_ before using it in malloc\nif (m_ > 0) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(size_t(m_));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    } else {\n        // Continue with program\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c4970af4-bd51-3e51-b65d-1e03641e91e4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of the block is specified in bytes. If the allocation is successful, a pointer to the block of memory is returned. If the allocation fails, a NULL pointer is returned. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size parameter passed to `malloc()` is not properly validated or is manipulated in such a way that it can cause an overflow or underflow condition. This can lead to unexpected behavior, including memory corruption, crashes, or potentially code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is important to validate all inputs that are used to determine the size of the memory allocation. This includes ensuring that the size is not zero, not excessively large, and not based on untrusted user input. \n\nAdditionally, it is important to check the return value of `malloc()` to ensure that the allocation was successful before using the memory. If `malloc()` returns a NULL pointer, this indicates that the allocation failed and the program should handle this error condition appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming 'that.m' is an integer representing the size\nif (that.m > 0) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(that.m);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle allocation failure\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c907bd25-6dfa-302b-98cb-9f92c4176a95",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of the block is specified in bytes. If the allocation is successful, a pointer to the block of memory is returned. If the allocation fails, a NULL pointer is returned. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size parameter passed to `malloc()` is not properly validated or is manipulated in such a way that it can cause an overflow or underflow condition. This can lead to unexpected behavior, including memory corruption, crashes, or potentially code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is important to validate all inputs that are used to determine the size of the memory allocation. This includes ensuring that the size is not zero, not excessively large, and not based on untrusted user input. \n\nAdditionally, it is important to check the return value of `malloc()` to ensure that the allocation was successful before using the memory. If `malloc()` returns a NULL pointer, this indicates that the allocation failed and the program should handle this error condition appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming 'that.m' is an integer representing the size\nif (that.m > 0) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(that.m);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle allocation failure\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
