            {
              "id": "1e8cb17a-345d-380c-9b38-6d642a0d23e4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question pertains to the incorrect usage of the `malloc` function in C++. `malloc` is a function that dynamically allocates memory during runtime. However, if not used correctly, it can lead to various vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, memory leaks, and null pointer dereferencing. In this case, the size of the memory to be allocated is determined by `that.size()`, which could potentially lead to a buffer overflow if `that.size()` returns a value larger than what can be handled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always validate the size of the memory to be allocated before calling `malloc`. This can be done by checking if the size is not larger than the maximum size that can be handled. Additionally, always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer, and any attempt to access such a pointer will result in undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for malloc and free\n#include <climits> // for UINT_MAX\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t size = that.size();\n\nif (size > UINT_MAX) {\n    // handle error\n}\n\ndense_vector_t = (dense_vector_type*) malloc(size * sizeof(dense_vector_type));\n\nif (dense_vector_t == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the size is not larger than `UINT_MAX`, which is the maximum value that can be represented by an unsigned int. Then, we check if `malloc` returned a null pointer, indicating that the memory allocation failed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This library provides functions for performing general functions, including dynamic memory management, random number generation, communication with the environment, integer arithmetics, searching, sorting and converting.\n- `<climits>`: This library is used to determine the limits of the integral types.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
